#
# 1. ITEM 7 (accounting).  Assertion failure, reported before the crash:
#      "not one freed buffer was reported CACHED or FREE -- whatis still
#       calls freed memory allocated"
#    umem_inspect.h documents UMEM_BUF_CACHED; umem_whatis() never returned it.
#
# 2. ITEM 1 (cache lifetime).  SIGSEGV. The faulting thread is inside
#    umem_findleaks()'s UNLOCKED cache-list walk, dereferencing a cache
#    descriptor, while another thread is destroying caches:
#
#      Thread 1 (faulting):
#        #0 umem_findleaks ... at umem_inspect.c:804
#           804   if ((cp->cache_cflags & (UMC_NOHASH | UMC_QCACHE)) == 0) {
#      Thread 6 (concurrent):
#        #1 umem_cache_destroy (cp=0x7f5196c71040) at umem.c:4884   <- munmap
#
#    That is exactly the defect: the walk followed cache_next without
#    umem_cache_lock while umem_cache_destroy() unlinked and freed caches.
#
# 3. ITEM 2 (no allocation/stdio under allocator locks).  Thread 2 shows stdio
#    running INSIDE the locked walk -- fputc under walk_allocated_cache, which
#    holds cache_lock:
#
#        #0 fputc
#        #1 json_escape          at umem_inspect.c:486
#        #2 walk_dump_cb         at umem_inspect.c:1303
#        #3 walk_allocated_cache at umem_inspect.c:301   <- holds cache_lock
#
#    Under malloc interposition that fputc re-enters the allocator and can
#    block on the lock the walk already holds.  It also shows the public
#    walker callback (walk_dump_cb) running under the lock.
#
# Post-fix, the same test passes (job insp3):
